Tests for every family after open items exercise only the library, never either binary (fa-y1vr fixes that).

Reality check 2026-10-14: still present on main, and wider than described. Besides the unbounded caller input (u64::MAX), corroboration is anchored to the daemon's OWN first reading.
- fad/clock.rs:106-123 records the first reading after every start uncorroborated, then treats later readings as corroborated when they are consistent with that first reading, not with true time. A real-time clock that starts years ahead therefore yields corroborated far-future ticks. The expiry sweep then expires every timed hold and the reversal sweep mints every scheduled reversal early, all journaled and immutable. After that, every real tick is refused with TICK_ORDER.
- A single UNcorroborated far-future tick also makes manual Posts on timed holds refuse HOLD_DUE, because post.rs:1843 uses the latest tick regardless of corroboration.
- Book::record_tick is public and accepts the caller's monotonic_ok.
Add to acceptance:
(a) corroboration requires an independent source (a second clock, a witness TimeEvidence, or the previous run's last corroborated tick plus elapsed monotonic time bounded by a registered max gap);
(b) a tick beyond a registered max skew is journaled as uncorroborated and authorizes no effects;
(c) an authorized TimeEvidence correction control (lineage, not a rewrite) recovers from a bad clock;
(d) HOLD_DUE uses the latest corroborated tick.
Lab tests: a daemon starting 5 years ahead produces no effects; one bad tick does not block manual Posts.

Slice 1 is implemented and gating, 2026-10-18 (claude-opus-5-5). It is a local commit on the fa-v62/fa-s7x stack and has not landed yet.

- **`fa --token <file>`:** acts as the holder of an issued token (fa-v62). `--as` and `--grant` beside it are refused (exit 2). A token that does not hold together (wrong secret, or principal not the holder) is refused before the book is opened. The session runs bound to the token's journaled capability, inside its launch template's ceiling.
- **`fad_request.token`:** an optional hex field. When present, fad binds that request to the token instead of running it in the owner posture. An absent or null token keeps the same-uid embedded-owner posture, so existing clients are unchanged.
- **The kernel** judges issuance and revocation at every admission. A token revoked mid-session is FA-REF-023 at its next write.
- **fa-mcp `--token` sessions** route to the book's fad when it holds the lease; a launch-config session still refuses that route.

Tests: a wire round trip; a CLI test of the direct route; a fad binary test with two holders through one daemon (attribution, cross-holder refusal, --as refusal, a tampered token, revocation).

Open in this bead (not slice 1):
- foreign-uid peers with tokens (`daemon.allowed_uids` and socket permissions);
- refusing `--as` on the same-uid owner route;
- a per-connection handshake (the token currently rides each request);
- `fa doctor` posture reporting;
- the lab_tests chaos run over the session loop.

TAKING (claude-opus-5-5, 2026-10-18): slice 1 of this bead. br refuses a formal claim while fa-llx blocks it. Scope:
(1) registries/capability_caveats.toml: the ops vocabulary, the caveat vocabulary with live/dormant status, and profile templates as data, checked against op.rs;
(2) IssueCapability as a journaled PolicyControl CapabilityEvent{kind=Issue} at a derived issuance identity, carrying the issued capability's canonical body;
(3) a holder-bound token: a possession caveat binds H(holder secret) into the capability commitment, and the token file is 0600;
(4) kernel: a capability carrying the possession caveat is admitted only with its journaled issuance (point witness, not revoked) and proof of possession;
(5) fa cap issue|list|show, and --token on the CLI and the fa-mcp launch.
The embedded-owner posture stays as-is and labelled. Please don't start a parallel implementation; I will comment with each landing sha.

Slice landed, 2026-10-16: an Agent may rehearse a trunk post (plan §15.3, "`fa post --dry-run` is implied for Agent-profile capabilities"). Before this, the default MCP Agent could not dry-run anything: admit_base refused AgentOnTrunk before the posting pipeline ran.

- **fa-policy:** admit_base_for_plan runs every admission check except the Agent trunk denial.
- **fa-journal:** prepare_plan runs the same pipeline. A planning PreparedPost refuses to publish. commit_plan still admits through fa_policy::admit, so an Agent plan is FA-REF-013 for the Agent and FA-REF-011 for anyone else (the plan's principal is the Agent).
- **fa-cli and fa-mcp:**
  - the bound predicate admits post, reverse, reserve and resolve with --dry-run for the Agent;
  - an Agent plan carries no commit next_action, and its warning says it commits for no one;
  - tool descriptions list "<verb> with dry_run=true".
- **Disclosed change:** the fa-mcp e2e default_agent_authority… test used to assert that an Agent cannot plan. It now asserts that dry_run=false is FA-REF-013 and that dry_run=true returns a plan with `book info` unchanged.

Not done (this bead stays open):
- the remaining Appendix B intent types;
- confirm_plan over MCP;
- PlanInvalidated carrying a fresh plan;
- plans bound to the accounting frontier (fa-1nx);
- an fa-reference plan oracle;
- FA-INV-11.core.

An Agent post without --dry-run stays the explicit FA-REF-013 refusal, not a silent conversion to a plan.
